  "assembly": "# Advent of Code 2025, day 12 (Christmas Tree Farm)\n#\n# THE PUZZLE\n# The input is a few shapes of presents, drawn on a grid of 3 by 3 (# is part of the present), and a\n# list of regions: a width, a length, and how many presents of each shape must go into it. Presents may\n# be turned and flipped, must lie on the grid and may not overlap. The answer is the number of regions\n# that can hold all their presents.\n#\n# THE IDEA\n# Deciding this in general means trying to pack, which takes far too long for regions of 40 by 40 with\n# 250 presents. But two simple tests are certain, each in one direction:\n#   - TOO MANY CELLS. Count the cells of all the presents. If that is more than WIDTH * LENGTH, they\n#     cannot fit, however they are turned.\n#   - ROOM FOR A BLOCK EACH. Cut the region into blocks of 3 by 3: (WIDTH / 3) * (LENGTH / 3) of them,\n#     dividing downwards. If there are at least as many blocks as presents, give every present a block\n#     of its own: they fit.\n# A region that passes neither test is UNDECIDED: the program does not guess. For the puzzle input every\n# region is decided by one of the two tests (573 have too many cells, 427 have room for a block each),\n# so FITTING is the answer. For the puzzle's small example all three regions are undecided: its answer, 2,\n# takes a real packing search, which this program does not have.\n#\n# THE WHOLE PROGRAM IN PSEUDO CODE\n#     for each shape: CELLS[shape] = the number of # in it\n#     for each region WIDTH, LENGTH and its counts:\n#         AREA = WIDTH * LENGTH\n#         NEED = the sum of count * CELLS[shape]; PRESENTS = the sum of the counts\n#         if NEED > AREA: TOO_MANY = TOO_MANY + 1\n#         else if (WIDTH / 3) * (LENGTH / 3) >= PRESENTS: FITTING = FITTING + 1\n#         else: UNDECIDED = UNDECIDED + 1\n#     write FITTING as five decimal digits\n#     stop\n# The CPU has no multiply and no divide. The numbers are small, so a product is made by adding one\n# number as often as the other says, and a division by 3 by counting how often 3 can be taken away.\n#\n# THE RESULT, when the CPU stops on the Breakpoint at END\n#   0x0100         FITTING: the regions that hold their presents for certain\n#   0x0101         UNDECIDED: the regions neither test decides (0 means FITTING is the puzzle's answer)\n#   0x0102         TOO_MANY: the regions that cannot hold them for certain\n#   0x0110-0x0114  FITTING as five decimal digits, one per word, most significant first\n#\n# THE INPUT (rule 1 of RULES.md), at 0x2000\n#     the shapes, in their order: one word per cell, 1 for # and 0 for .; 0xFFFE ends a row of the\n#     drawing and 0xFFFF the shape. After the last shape comes one more 0xFFFF.\n#     the regions: the width, the length, the count for each shape, then 0xFFFF. After the last region\n#     comes one more 0xFFFF.\n# Built-in limits: shapes of at most 3 by 3 (the block test relies on it); at most 256 shapes; every\n# number below 65,536, also WIDTH * LENGTH and the cells needed.\n#\n# ONE WORD IS ENOUGH\n# Every value here fits in 16 bits, so each variable is one memory word. The idioms used everywhere\n# (v is a variable's address, below 0x1000):\n#     getA v                   A = the word at v   (lia v; load rgA rgA: lia puts the address in A, load replaces it)\n#     getB v                   B = the word at v\n#     putA v                   the word at v = A   (this uses up B)\n#     getAat p / putAat p      the same for the word the pointer p points at\n#     copy v w                 w = v\n#     if_eq L / if_neq L       compares A with B as they are now\n#     sub; if_carry L          after A - B: taken when A >= B\n# What each instruction does exactly is in findings/cpu-instructions.md.\n#\n# MEMORY MAP\n#   0x0100-0x013F  variables            0x0200-0x02FF  CELLS: the number of cells of each shape\n#   0x1000-        the program          0x1E00-  POWERS: 10000, 1000, 100, 10, 1, 0\n#   0x2000-        the input (6 shapes and 1,000 regions end at 0x4371)\n#   0xFF00         where the CPU starts\n\n# ---- The CPU's instructions as symbols (day 1's table without its _32 names, which the CPU ignores) ----\nsymbol nop 0b0000_0000_0000_0000            # the word 0: stops the CPU (the same word as non)\nsymbol ld  0b0010_0000_0000_0000            # load a register from memory (used through the load macro)\nsymbol lia 0b0011_0000_0000_0000            # lia n: A = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol lib 0b0100_0000_0000_0000            # lib n: B = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol sto 0b0101_0000_0000_0000            # store a register to memory (used through the store macro)\n\nmacro  load  { ld  | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # load X Y: X = the word at the address in Y\nmacro  store { sto | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # store X Y: the word at the address in Y = X\n\nsymbol sum    0b0110_0100_0000_0000         # A = A + B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol sub    0b0110_0010_0000_0000         # A = A - B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol shf    0b0110_0001_0000_0000         # shf | k: A = A shifted left by k (0 to 15)\nsymbol and    0b0110_0000_1000_0000         # A = A and B\nsymbol or     0b0110_0000_0100_0000         # A = A or B\nsymbol xor    0b0110_0000_0010_0000         # A = A xor B\nsymbol neg    0b0110_0000_0001_0000         # A = every bit of A flipped\n\nsymbol psh 0b1010_0000_0000_0000            # psh X: push the 16 bits of X\nsymbol pop 0b1011_0000_0000_0000            # pop X: pop 16 bits into X\n\nsymbol jmp  0b1100_0000_0000_0000           # jmp; L: go to L.  jmp c; L: go to L when c holds\nsymbol call 0b1110_0000_0000_0000           # call; L: run the subroutine at L\nsymbol ret  0b1111_0000_0000_0000           # return from a subroutine\n\n# The registers, as load, store, psh and pop name them\nsymbol rgAH 0b1000_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgA  0b0100_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgBH 0b0010_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgB  0b0001_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgSP 0b0000_1000_0000                # the program counter (leave alone)\nsymbol rgSB 0b0000_0100_0000                # the stack pointer (leave alone)\nsymbol non  0                               # stops the CPU (Breakpoint): the end of the program\n\n# The conditions of jmp\nsymbol zero  0b1000_0000_0000               # the last sum or sub gave 0\nsymbol carry 0b0100_0000_0000               # the last sum carried, or the last sub did not borrow (A >= B)\nsymbol nega  0b0010_0000_0000               # the last sub went below 0 (A < B)\nsymbol eq    0b0001_0000_0000               # A == B right now\nsymbol neq   0b0000_1000_0000               # A != B right now\n\n# ---- Macros for the common operations (explained in the README). A macro cannot use another macro, so\n#      load and store are spelled out here. v: a variable, p: a variable holding an address, L: a label ----\nmacro getA     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                                              # A = v\nmacro getB     { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6) }                                              # B = v\nmacro putA     { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                                             # v = A (uses B)\nmacro getAat   { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                       # A = the word p points at\nmacro putAat   { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6); sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # the word p points at = A (uses B)\nmacro inc      { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib 1; sum; lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }  # v = v + 1 (uses A and B)\nmacro goto     { jmp; a? }                                                                    # go to L\nmacro if_eq    { jmp eq; a? }                                                                 # go to L when A == B\nmacro do       { call; a? }                                                                   # call the subroutine L\nmacro if_carry { jmp carry; a? }                                                              # go to L after a sub when A >= B\nmacro copy     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # w = v (uses A and B)\nmacro if_neq   { jmp neq; a? }                                                                # go to L when A != B\n\n# ---- Variables (all below 0x1000, so lia/lib can address them) ----\n# The results\npointer FITTING 0x0100 0\npointer UNDECIDED 0x0101 0\npointer TOO_MANY 0x0102 0\n# IN: the address of the next word of the input. SC: a place in CELLS. COUNT: a number being counted\npointer IN 0x0120 0x2000\npointer SC 0x0121 0x0200\npointer COUNT 0x0122 0\n# The region: its sides, their product, the cells needed, the number of presents\npointer WIDTH 0x0123 0\npointer LENGTH 0x0124 0\npointer AREA 0x0125 0\npointer NEED 0x0126 0\npointer PRESENTS 0x0127 0\n# TIMES: how often something is still to be added. NUMBER: a count of presents from the input\npointer TIMES 0x0128 0\npointer NUMBER 0x0129 0\n# W3, L3: WIDTH / 3 and LENGTH / 3. BLOCKS: their product. LEFT: what is left of a side while dividing\npointer W3 0x012A 0\npointer L3 0x012B 0\npointer BLOCKS 0x012C 0\npointer LEFT 0x012D 0\n# For the decimal digits: what is left of FITTING, the current power of ten, how often it fitted\npointer REST 0x012E 0\npointer POWER 0x012F 0\npointer DIGIT_VALUE 0x0130 0\n# Pointers: DIGIT the next digit to write, POW the next power of ten\npointer DIGIT 0x0131 0x0110\npointer POW 0x0132 0x1E00\n# Constants that do not fit an instruction's place for a number\npointer CELLS 0x0133 0x0200\npointer END_MARK 0x0134 0xFFFF\n\n# ---- The CPU starts at 0xFF00 ----\norigin 0xFF00\ngoto MAIN\n\norigin 0x1000\n@ MAIN\n# ==== the shapes: count their cells ====\n#     while memory[IN] != 0xFFFF:                           # SC starts at CELLS\n#         COUNT = 0\n#         while memory[IN] != 0xFFFF:\n#             if memory[IN] == 1: COUNT = COUNT + 1         # a 0 or the 0xFFFE that ends a row counts nothing\n#             IN = IN + 1\n#         memory[SC] = COUNT; SC = SC + 1; IN = IN + 1\n#     IN = IN + 1\n@ SHAPE\n\tgetAat IN; getB END_MARK                                     # A = memory[IN], B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq SHAPES_DONE                                            # the end of the shapes\n\tlia 0; putA COUNT                                            # COUNT = 0\n@ SHAPE_CELL\n\tgetAat IN; getB END_MARK                                     # A = memory[IN], B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq SHAPE_END                                              # the end of this shape\n\tlib 1                                                        # B = 1\n\tif_neq SHAPE_NEXT                                            # not part of the present\n\tinc COUNT                                                    # COUNT = COUNT + 1\n@ SHAPE_NEXT\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1\n\tgoto SHAPE_CELL\n@ SHAPE_END\n\tgetA COUNT; putAat SC                                        # memory[SC] = COUNT\n\tinc SC                                                       # SC = SC + 1\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1: past the 0xFFFF\n\tgoto SHAPE\n@ SHAPES_DONE\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1: past the 0xFFFF\n\n# ==== a region: its area ====\n#     WIDTH = memory[IN]; if WIDTH == 0xFFFF: goto ALL_DONE\n#     LENGTH = memory[IN + 1]; IN = IN + 2\n#     AREA = 0; repeat LENGTH times: AREA = AREA + WIDTH\n@ REGION\n\tgetAat IN; putA WIDTH                                        # WIDTH = memory[IN]\n\tgetB END_MARK                                                # B = 0xFFFF (A still holds WIDTH)\n\tif_eq ALL_DONE                                               # the end of the regions\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1\n\tload rgA rgA; putA LENGTH                                    # LENGTH = memory[IN] (A still held IN)\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1\n\tlia 0; putA AREA                                             # AREA = 0\n\tcopy LENGTH TIMES                                            # TIMES = LENGTH\n@ AREA_ADD\n\tgetA TIMES; lib 0                                            # A = TIMES, B = 0\n\tif_eq AREA_DONE                                              # WIDTH has been added LENGTH times\n\tlib 1; sub; putA TIMES                                       # TIMES = TIMES - 1\n\tgetA AREA; getB WIDTH; sum; putA AREA                        # AREA = AREA + WIDTH\n\tgoto AREA_ADD\n@ AREA_DONE\n\n# ==== its presents ====\n#     NEED = 0; PRESENTS = 0\n#     for SC = CELLS; memory[IN] != 0xFFFF; SC = SC + 1, IN = IN + 1:\n#         NUMBER = memory[IN]; PRESENTS = PRESENTS + NUMBER\n#         repeat memory[SC] times: NEED = NEED + NUMBER     # NUMBER * (the cells of the shape)\n#     IN = IN + 1\n\tlia 0; putA NEED; putA PRESENTS                              # NEED = 0; PRESENTS = 0\n\tcopy CELLS SC                                                # SC = the first shape\n@ PRESENT\n\tgetAat IN; putA NUMBER                                       # NUMBER = memory[IN]\n\tgetB END_MARK                                                # B = 0xFFFF (A still holds NUMBER)\n\tif_eq PRESENTS_DONE                                          # the end of the counts\n\tgetB PRESENTS; sum; putA PRESENTS                            # PRESENTS = PRESENTS + NUMBER\n\tgetAat SC; putA TIMES                                        # TIMES = the cells of this shape\n@ NEED_ADD\n\tgetA TIMES; lib 0                                            # A = TIMES, B = 0\n\tif_eq NEED_DONE                                              # NUMBER has been added once for every cell\n\tlib 1; sub; putA TIMES                                       # TIMES = TIMES - 1\n\tgetA NEED; getB NUMBER; sum; putA NEED                       # NEED = NEED + NUMBER\n\tgoto NEED_ADD\n@ NEED_DONE\n\tinc SC                                                       # SC = SC + 1\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1\n\tgoto PRESENT\n@ PRESENTS_DONE\n\tinc IN                                                       # IN = IN + 1: past the 0xFFFF\n\n# ==== the two tests ====\n#     if NEED > AREA: TOO_MANY = TOO_MANY + 1; goto REGION\n#     W3 = WIDTH / 3; L3 = LENGTH / 3                       # how often 3 can be taken away\n#     BLOCKS = 0; repeat L3 times: BLOCKS = BLOCKS + W3\n#     if BLOCKS >= PRESENTS: FITTING = FITTING + 1\n#     else: UNDECIDED = UNDECIDED + 1\n\tgetA AREA; getB NEED; sub                                    # A = AREA - NEED\n\tif_carry ENOUGH_CELLS                                        # AREA >= NEED\n\tinc TOO_MANY                                                 # TOO_MANY = TOO_MANY + 1\n\tgoto REGION\n@ ENOUGH_CELLS\n\tlia 0; putA W3; putA L3; putA BLOCKS                         # W3 = 0; L3 = 0; BLOCKS = 0\n\tcopy WIDTH LEFT                                              # LEFT = WIDTH\n@ WIDTH_BY_3\n\tgetA LEFT; lib 3; sub                                        # A = LEFT - 3\n\tif_carry WIDTH_TAKE                                          # LEFT >= 3\n\tgoto WIDTH_DIVIDED\n@ WIDTH_TAKE\n\tputA LEFT                                                    # LEFT = LEFT - 3 (A still holds the difference)\n\tinc W3                                                       # W3 = W3 + 1\n\tgoto WIDTH_BY_3\n@ WIDTH_DIVIDED\n\tcopy LENGTH LEFT                                             # LEFT = LENGTH\n@ LENGTH_BY_3\n\tgetA LEFT; lib 3; sub                                        # A = LEFT - 3\n\tif_carry LENGTH_TAKE                                         # LEFT >= 3\n\tgoto LENGTH_DIVIDED\n@ LENGTH_TAKE\n\tputA LEFT                                                    # LEFT = LEFT - 3\n\tinc L3                                                       # L3 = L3 + 1\n\tgetA BLOCKS; getB W3; sum; putA BLOCKS                       # BLOCKS = BLOCKS + W3: one more row of blocks\n\tgoto LENGTH_BY_3\n@ LENGTH_DIVIDED\n\tgetA BLOCKS; getB PRESENTS; sub                              # A = BLOCKS - PRESENTS\n\tif_carry BLOCK_EACH                                          # BLOCKS >= PRESENTS\n\tinc UNDECIDED                                                # UNDECIDED = UNDECIDED + 1\n\tgoto REGION\n@ BLOCK_EACH\n\tinc FITTING                                                  # FITTING = FITTING + 1\n\tgoto REGION\n\n# ==== write FITTING as five decimal digits ====\n# A digit is how often a power of ten fits into what is left. Without division that is counting:\n#     REST = FITTING\n#     for POWER in 10000, 1000, 100, 10, 1:\n#         DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n#         while REST >= POWER: REST = REST - POWER; DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n#         memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE; DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n# After sub, the carry flag is set when A >= B (no borrow).\n@ ALL_DONE\n\tgetA FITTING; putA REST                                        # REST = FITTING\n@ NEXT_POWER\n\tgetAat POW                                                   # A = memory[POW], the next power of ten\n\tputA POWER                                                   # POWER = A\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq END                                                    # a power of 0 ends the table\n\tinc POW                                                      # POW = POW + 1\n\tlia 0; putA DIGIT_VALUE                                      # DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n@ FITS_AGAIN\n\tgetA REST; getB POWER                                        # A = REST, B = POWER\n\tsub                                                          # A = REST - POWER\n\tif_carry FITS                                                # REST >= POWER: it fits once more\n\tgetA DIGIT_VALUE; putAat DIGIT                                       # memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE   (B holds the address in DIGIT)\n\tinc DIGIT                                                    # DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_POWER\n@ FITS\n\tputA REST                                                    # REST = REST - POWER   (A still holds the difference)\n\tinc DIGIT_VALUE                                                          # DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n\tgoto FITS_AGAIN\n\n# The word 0 stops the CPU on its Breakpoint.\n@ END\n\tnon\n# ---- Powers of ten for the decimal digits, then 0 ----\norigin 0x1E00\n10000; 1000; 100; 10; 1; 0\n\n\n# ---- The shapes, then the regions ----\norigin 0x2000\n# shape 0: ### / ..# / ###\n1; 1; 1; 0xFFFE; 0; 0; 1; 0xFFFE; 1; 1; 1; 0xFFFF\n# shape 1: ..# / .## / ##.\n0; 0; 1; 0xFFFE; 0; 1; 1; 0xFFFE; 1; 1; 0; 0xFFFF\n# shape 2: #.. / ### / ###\n1; 0; 0; 0xFFFE; 1; 1; 1; 0xFFFE; 1; 1; 1; 0xFFFF\n# shape 3: ### / ##. / .##\n1; 1; 1; 0xFFFE; 1; 1; 0; 0xFFFE; 0; 1; 1; 0xFFFF\n# shape 4: #.# / ### / #.#\n1; 0; 1; 0xFFFE; 1; 1; 1; 0xFFFE; 1; 0; 1; 0xFFFF\n# shape 5: ..# / .## / ###\n0; 0; 1; 0xFFFE; 0; 1; 1; 0xFFFE; 1; 1; 1; 0xFFFF\n0xFFFF\n# the regions: width; length; the counts; 0xFFFF\n41; 38; 26; 26; 29; 23; 21; 30; 0xFFFF\n40; 38; 17; 28; 31; 34; 26; 19; 0xFFFF\n46; 42; 39; 32; 32; 33; 40; 33; 0xFFFF\n48; 42; 43; 49; 68; 43; 59; 47; 0xFFFF\n48; 41; 33; 36; 32; 40; 32; 35; 0xFFFF\n40; 42; 48; 43; 44; 40; 40; 44; 0xFFFF\n42; 39; 39; 28; 37; 20; 34; 24; 0xFFFF\n48; 35; 46; 35; 32; 56; 46; 41; 0xFFFF\n40; 44; 49; 41; 35; 54; 44; 47; 0xFFFF\n38; 47; 24; 31; 25; 29; 41; 29; 0xFFFF\n39; 40; 29; 28; 25; 27; 30; 29; 0xFFFF\n44; 42; 60; 52; 31; 45; 51; 47; 0xFFFF\n49; 46; 38; 37; 41; 50; 33; 41; 0xFFFF\n46; 35; 39; 38; 32; 38; 47; 55; 0xFFFF\n43; 37; 27; 29; 26; 25; 35; 25; 0xFFFF\n48; 48; 51; 35; 39; 56; 37; 38; 0xFFFF\n41; 36; 41; 41; 43; 30; 42; 30; 0xFFFF\n43; 47; 33; 28; 41; 30; 37; 41; 0xFFFF\n41; 35; 24; 33; 21; 23; 20; 21; 0xFFFF\n46; 50; 34; 50; 34; 47; 40; 35; 0xFFFF\n50; 47; 45; 35; 36; 33; 44